WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.328 --> 00:00:03.245
(crowd chattering)

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.900
- [Reporter] Kabul's international airport

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.450
continues to be overwhelmed

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:09.590
with people trying to flee Afghanistan

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.613
since the Taliban took over
the capital on August 15th.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.230
As scenes of desperation have played out,

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.100
the Taliban have
threatened to block Afghans

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.380
from leaving the country,

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.030
while President Biden has
doubled down on his deadline

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:25.620
for when U.S. troops are
scheduled to leave the country.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:30.400
- We are currently on a pace
to finish by August the 31st,

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.830
the sooner we can finish the better.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:36.363
Each day of operations brings
added risk to our troops.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:38.880
- [Reporter] As the deadline looms,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.010
Afghans able to board evacuation flights

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.310
face long journeys to
their host countries,

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.193
starting a resettlement
process with challenges ahead.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.890
The path to getting out of
the country hasn't been clear.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.930
One huge hurdle, documentation.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.960
- It sounds like from people
that we've interviewed,

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.760
that it mostly comes down to luck,

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.200
whether you get into the airport or not.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.210
The first few days of
the evacuation effort

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:03.440
it was kind of so chaotic.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.640
They were letting a lot of people in,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.440
including people who didn't
really have valid visa

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.080
or travel documents.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.120
And now we're kind of seeing the reverse,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.290
people even who do have
valid American visas

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.500
are going up to the gates of the airport,

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.840
waving them at American
troops or Canadian troops

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.083
and still not getting into the airport.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.620
- [Reporter] Once they are inside,

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.150
issues with document
verification continue,

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.200
adding to the bottleneck.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.100
- But a lot of cases,

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.500
you had people with fake,
or even no documents,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.360
getting into the airport
in the first few days,

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.010
cramming themselves onto planes,
getting out of Afghanistan.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:46.220
And now partially because
all that happened,

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.830
the U.S. is really prioritizing
getting American citizens

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.760
and American green card
holders through that gate,

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.524
and it means more Afghans
are being left behind.

00:01:54.524 --> 00:01:56.740
(crowd chattering)

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.070
- [Reporter] And there's added urgency,

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.020
as the Taliban have threatened retaliation

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.550
against Americans and others

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.890
if the U.S. remains past the deadline.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.820
Late Wednesday, the U.S. Embassy urged

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.880
Americans outside the airport's
gates to leave immediately

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.550
citing security threats.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.280
By that point, the U.S. and its allies

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.620
had evacuated more than 88,000 people

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:18.230
since the Taliban closed
in on Kabul on August 14th.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.610
Military transport planes
have shoveled evacuees

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.150
to hubs in Qatar, Bahrain, and Germany,

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.333
with smaller settler hubs
receiving evacuees as well.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.300
These locations have been
setting up temporary beds

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.970
and tents to receive evacuees

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.010
who, in some cases, are in the
middle of getting their visas

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.090
to come to the U.S..

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:37.923
- [Michelle] They have to go through

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:40.370
a pretty rigorous security process

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.120
that could sometimes take up to a year,

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.650
and so they'll need to live
in those third countries

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.090
for that duration while
they're waiting for their visas

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:47.923
to come through.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.810
- [Reporter] There are
currently a few pathways

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.440
for entry into the U.S..

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.760
Evacuees with visas,

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.450
or those who are in the visa
application process can enter.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:58.770
People who don't have visas

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.510
are often sent to a third country

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.960
while a security screening is completed.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.860
Those who work for an American non-profit

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.400
or media organization,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.010
or individuals facing
political, religious,

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:12.180
or ethnic persecution
can enter as refugees.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.800
There's also a fourth option,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.160
an immigration program known as parole,

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.330
which gives evacuees temporary permission

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.170
to live and work in the U.S..

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.670
Once they're in the country,
they can apply for asylum,

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:24.800
possibly get sponsorship from family,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.950
or wait for their visa to come through.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.280
Those arriving in the U.S.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.470
are initially being housed in
military bases in New Jersey,

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.750
Virginia, Wisconsin, and Texas,

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.123
while other bases may eventually
receive evacuees as well.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.120
- [Michelle] They're kind of being used

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.490
as reception centers in some cases,

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.220
we have to do medical screenings on them

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.460
before they can get their visas.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.630
In some cases, they're
just being connected

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:50.310
with resettlement organizations
in the United States

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.773
who are helping them get
connected to services here.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.550
- [Reporter] Many of these
resettlement organizations

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.770
are contracted with the U.S. government.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.960
The groups help evacuees
find a place to live

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.560
and enroll their children in schools

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.363
with the aim of integrating
them into American life.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.400
- [Michelle] Anyone who gets here

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.210
has been through an extremely
harrowing experience,

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.802
and we have to remember,
those are the lucky ones.

00:04:13.802 --> 00:04:16.719
(crowd chattering)

